  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 219 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThrough mid-late next week (Thursday)...\n\nThrough this weekend: The Gulf Coast states remain entrenched \nunder a sprawling high at the surface and aloft (598 to 600DM). \nRAP analysis/water vapor imagery indicate the center of mid level \nhigh pressure gradually shifting westward today, leading anomalous\nwarmth, some 8-15F above normal (95F to 101F). Drier air is \nevident in GOES East total PWs (0.9 to 1.3 inches) moving across \nthe Gulf Coast region as the mid level high shifts westward. While\nit will remain hot, dangerous heat stress will be somewhat \ndecreased and shifted northward. Lows will be seasonably warm, a \ntouch cooler this morning in the mid to upper 60s to the southeast\n(66-70F along and southeast of the Natchez Trace) while low 70s \n(71-74F) to the northwest into the Delta, before a touch warmer \ntonight (69-74F).\n\nHighs continue to moderate into the weekend, with an influx of \ndeeper moisture. A westward propagating upper low/surface trough \nover the northeast Gulf will lead to an influx of deeper moisture \naloft and higher boundary layer moisture, which supports less \nmixing and more concerns of dangerous heat. Highs will moderate \ninto the upper 90s to around 100F (97-102F), with 101-102F \npossible, especially in northern observation sites. Added some \ntargeted heat graphics in the HWO for today into the weekend \n(Limited north of Interstate 20 today, areawide Limited Saturday, \nand Elevated west of Interstate 20 late week on Sunday). Holding \noff on any heat headlines for now. Rain chances remain a low \nchance (less than 15 percent), with any chances possible southeast\nof the Natchez Trace into the Interstate 59 corridors. While the \nofficial forecast indicate a low chance of rain (less than 15 \npercent), some consensus and synoptic pattern could support higher\ncoverage than indicated, as much as isolated/low chance rain and \nstorm coverage in the Pine Belt late weekend on Sunday (15-30 \npercent).\n\nNext week (Through next Thursday): As the upper low weakens and \nanother shortwave/cold core low propagates into the Mid West \nStates through middle to late next week, the mid level feature \nover the northeast Gulf is progged to lift northward. This feature\nwill become absorbed in the mean flow to the north, which may \nbecome more amplified than previously anticipated (per deep cold \ncore over the Great Lakes in 18/00Z Euro). This will lead to less \nfocused moisture convergence but persistent deep moisture (PWs \naround 1.75 to greater than 2 inches).There are continued \nindications of weak front/convergence zone and northwesterly flow \nsinking into the area early next week, with drier air and lower \ndewpoints late week. \n\nDangerous heat concerns will persist into early week and will \ncontinue to message Elevated HWO heat graphics for Monday, less \noppressive Tuesday and much more pleasant into late week. Combined\nwith the ridge less stout and centered over east TX, highs will \nnot be as seasonably warm later (10F to 16F above early week to 4F\nto 12F above late week). Highs peak in the 96F to 101F range \nearly week to 88F to 96F by mid to late week. Rain and storm \nchances remain in the isolated/low chance range (15-25 percent) on\naverage in east to southeast MS, with deep moisture/frontal \nconvergence possibly driving coverage a touch higher in the \nscattered/medium chance range (20 to 40 percent range). There \ncould be some cooler thermal profiles aloft, with vertical totals \nin the mid to upper 20s (26C to 28C); isolated strong storms \ncannot be ruled out at times. /DC/",
